1
00:00:01,020 --> 00:00:07,080
Now, I will briefly state what Ramanuja's unique contribution
was and conclude my talk.

2
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:11,020
What happened was...

3
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:19,000
...around 2,500 years ago, the Buddha came, and Mahavira came.
Back then, they...

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,000
...propagated their respective teachings.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,000
While they were propagating...

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,000
...several of the ideas they introduced were contrary to the Vedas.

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:38,000
These anti-Vedic ideas became even more pronounced around 1,500
years ago, when we see among Buddhists...

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,000
...the emergence of the four major schools: Sautrantika, Vaibhashika,
Yogachara, and Madhyamika...

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,000
...making their stance even more rigorous.

10
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Within those, the Yogachara and Madhyamika Buddhists concluded that...

11
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:05,000
...everything we perceive in reality is not true at all;
rather, everything is an illusion (mithya).

12
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,000
When asked what the basis of this illusion is, the Madhyamikas
asserted it is shunya (emptiness/void).

13
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:21,000
The Yogacharas agreed it is void, but held that what we perceive is relatively real.
Thus, the Buddhists formulated this jagan-mithya-vada.

14
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Later on, we began to see the very same ideas appearing among
orthodox (Astika) thinkers—for example, Gaudapada, Shankara...

15
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:35,000
...Mandana Mishra, and Abhinavagupta.

16
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Among all of them, we find the very same concepts that had previously
emerged among the Buddhists.

17
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Hence, we see a close resemblance between them. Because...

18
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,000
...just as the Buddhists claim that the Vedas, the Puranas,
and our entire experience are all illusory...

19
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:57,000
...similarly, Shankaracharya, Gaudapada...

20
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,000
...and later Advaitins also assert the same.

21
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:11,000
They maintain that the world is an illusion, and that the Vedas and
Puranas are not ultimately real (paramarthika), but are actually illusory.

22
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Therefore, on this point, there is a clear parallel between the Buddhists
and the Advaitins. We can see that this is highly contrary to the Vedas.

23
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Now, there is no rule that we must automatically reject whatever
the Buddha has said.

24
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,000
If what they said is aligned with the Vedas and our own experience,
we can certainly accept it.

25
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:44,000
But what we observe here is contradictory, as I will briefly explain.

26
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,000
But most importantly...

27
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:56,000
...Gaudapada himself, for example, admits this in his
Gaudapada Karikas:

28
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:02,000
"Naitad Buddhena bhashitam." Similarly, in Anandagiri's
Shankara Vijaya...

29
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:06,000
...when Shankara meets Kumarila Bhatta...

30
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:13,000
...he is addressed as, "Ah, so another Buddhist has arrived."

31
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Shankara replies, "No, I am not a Buddhist."
Similarly, later commentators like Bhaskara...

32
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:26,000
...Ramanuja, and Madhva also recognize this parallel
in these philosophies.

33
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:32,000
They note that the Buddhist concept of Nirvishesha-vada
(attributeless reality)...

34
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:42,000
...and the claim of an attributeless entity are common to both,
and subsequent Acharyas argue that this is not found in the Vedas.

35
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
In this matter, as I have mentioned, there are several difficulties.

36
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
First, there is a logical error or contradiction (yukti-virodha).

37
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,000
For instance, in our day-to-day experience...

38
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:05,000
...not a single person in their life has ever perceived
a completely attributeless object (nirvishesha vastu).

39
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:10,000
When I see a face or hear a sound, there is always some specific
attribute (vishesha) present.

40
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Be it the tone of voice, the language, or the facial features—
some distinction is always there.

41
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,000
This holds true even in our dreams.

42
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:24,000
We can even demonstrate that it exists in deep sleep (sushupti),
though I won't delve too deeply into that now.

43
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Similarly, regarding the concept of Avidya (ignorance)...

44
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
...they claim it is beginningless (anadi).

45
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:38,000
They also state that Brahman is eternal and attributeless.

46
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:41,000
Thus, one might ask: what is the locus of this Avidya?

47
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:45,000
But let me leave the topic of Avidya here for now.

48
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:55,000
We can also point out other flaws. For instance, from where
do they derive this theory?

49
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:06,000
In ancient times, concepts like Rajju-Sarpa-Nyaya (rope-snake analogy)
are not found in the early Vedas; they were adopted much later.

50
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:13,000
If we look at it objectively, this analogy is not found
anywhere in the ancient Upanishads.

51
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:25,000
It only appears in texts from about 1,500 years ago,
specifically in Buddhist and Yoga texts.

52
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Thus, these logical flaws exist, along with flaws in hermeneutics
(Mimamsa-dosha).

53
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:35,000
When interpreting great statements (Mahavakyas) like "Aham Brahmasmi"
or "Tat Tvam Asi"...

54
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:44,000
...Advaitins must abandon the literal meaning (abhidha) and resort
to metaphorical meaning (lakshana). Madhvas also do this differently.

55
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:51,000
This hermeneutical flaw is effectively resolved by Ramanuja.

56
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:57,000
When such flaws enter philosophical inquiry...

57
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:01,000
...another issue is Shankara's Vaishnava stance.

58
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:11,000
In his commentary on the Prasthanatrayi (Upanishads, Gita, Brahma Sutras),
which are genuinely recognized as his own works...

59
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:17,000
...Shankara consistently identifies Vishnu as the Supreme Brahman
(Parabrahman) or Saguna Brahman.

60
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:19,000
He does not elevate other deities.

61
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:29,000
However, later Advaitins superimposed other texts onto him
and presented a different view.

62
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:33,000
To address all these issues, our Ramanujacharya...

63
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:41,000
...systematically points out and resolves these flaws.

64
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:48,000
Therefore, as stated in the verse I recited at the very beginning
of this talk:

65
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:57,000
"Salutations to that Ramanuja, the supreme yogi,
who cooled the inner fever of the Vedas, Smritis, and Sutras." [1]

66
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:05,000
The Buddhist-influenced ideas that did not align with the Vedas,
Smritis, or Sutras...

67
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:16,000
...were superimposed upon them, and Ramanuja diagnosed
and cured this "fever."

68
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:22,000
Thus, offering my respects once again to all three Acharyas...

69
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:29,000
...we must emulate their sincere quest for ultimate truth.

70
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:33,000
We should endeavor to see the truth objectively and impartially.

71
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:41,000
Setting aside our personal biases, we must desire only the truth.
With these words...

72
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:51,000
...and offering my gratitude to all the elders who gave me this
opportunity, I conclude my talk here. Thank you.
